He came to his disciples and said to them: “Brother, do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?”
Answer them: “Every tree that my father didn’t plant in the sky will be uprooted.”
Stay away from them! They are blind leading the blind. And if a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.”
And they said to Him, “Explain to us this parable.”
And Jesus said: “My brothers, do you still not understand?”
Brother, don't you see that anything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach, and then comes out from the body?
As for the need to express what’s in the mouth, it comes from the heart, and this is what corrupts a person.
From the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, corruption, theft, false testimony, and disbelief.
These are the things that defile a person. But eating with unwashed hands does not defile a person.” (The woman’s faith, a Canaanite woman – Mark 7:24-30)
And Jesus walked from that place and came to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
And a woman from that region came, crying out, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter is possessed by a demon, and she suffers greatly.”
As he didn't even answer her with a word. Then his students came and begged him, and they said: "Give her to whoever you love, let her leave us alone, she's following us and screaming!"
Jesus answered: “I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.”
As she came, she dismounted before him on her knees and said: “My lord, help me!”
And he replied: “It’s not good to take the children’s bread and throw it to the dogs.”
She said: “Yes sir! Even dogs eat the leftovers that fall from their owner’s table.”
Jesus replied to her, “Woman, your faith is great! Be it done for you as you wish.” And at that moment, the woman was healed. Jesus healed many people.
And Jesus went on from there and passed along by the Sea of Galilee. And he went up into the mountain and sat down there.
And a large number of people came to him, bringing with them paralyzed people, the blind, the lame, and those afflicted with skin diseases, and many others, and they placed them before his feet, and he healed them.
And the people were amazed, they discovered the lepers speaking, and the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel. Jesus fed four thousand men (Mark 8:1-10)
And Jesus called his disciples and said to them: “The people are bothering me, because they have stayed with me for three days without eating. And I do not want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way.”
And the disciples said to Him, “Where shall we get bread in this desolate place to satisfy such a large crowd?”
And He asked them, “How many loaves do you have?” They said, “Seven loaves and a few small fish.”
And Jesus ordered the people to sit on the ground,
And He took the seven loaves and the small fish, and gave thanks and broke them, and gave them to His disciples, and the disciples gave them to the people.
And they all ate until they were full, and they carried away what was left, seven large baskets full.
To Klaw, they were four thousand men, not counting women and children.
And after sending the people off, he went out in the boat and headed to the Majdal area.
Signs of the times (Mark 8:11-13; Luke 12:54-56)
Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came to Jesus and tested him, asking him to show them a sign from heaven.
And Jesus answered them, “When evening comes, you say, ‘The weather is fine, because the sky is red.’”
In the morning, you say: "There's rain today, because the sky is a dark red." You know how to interpret the state of the sky, but you don't know how to interpret the signs of the times.
A wicked and adulterous generation asks for a sign, but only a sign will be given to them—the sign of Jonah.” And he left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees (Mark 8:14-21)
They got the students to the other bank, they forgot to bring the bread with them.
Then Jesus said to them, “Be careful and watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”
And the students discussed among themselves and said: “That’s because we don’t have bread.”
And Jesus knew this and said: “You with little faith, why do you discuss among yourselves and say, ‘We have no bread’?”
Brother, don't you understand yet? Brother, don't you remember the five loaves of bread that were distributed among the five thousand and how many large baskets were left over when they had enough?
And the seven loaves of bread for the four thousand, and how many baskets were filled?
How is it that you don’t understand that I’m not talking about bread? Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees!”
At that time they understood that He had not warned them to beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees, but of their teaching. Peter confessed that Jesus is the Christ (Mark 8:27-30; Luke 9:18-21).
And it came to pass, when Jesus was come to the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, “Who do men say that I the Son of man am?”
They replied: “Some say you are John the Baptist, others that you are Elijah, and still others that you are Jeremiah or one of the prophets.”
He said to them: “So, who do you say I am?”
Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”
And they said to Jesus: “Health to you, Simon son of Jonah! Not flesh and blood has shown you this thing, but rather the Father who is in heaven.”
And I tell you: You are Peter, which means a large rock, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell will not overcome it.
Then I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”
And at that time He commanded His disciples not to tell anyone that He was the Christ. Jesus reveals for the first time about His death and resurrection (Mark 8:31; 9:1; Luke 9:22-27).
And after this time began Jesus to show to His disciples that He must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes of the Jews, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.
And Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke Him, saying, “Far be it from You, Lord! This shall never happen to You!”
And Jesus turned and said to Peter, “Get behind me, Satan! You are a hindrance to me, for you are thinking as humans do, not as God does.”
At that time Jesus said to his disciples: “If anyone wants to follow me, let him deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.”
Whoever wants to end his life loses it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will gain it.
What is the use of a person if they win the whole world and lose their life? Or what is something a person would trade their life for?
I am coming as the Son of Man with my angels in the glory of my Father, and at that time I will reward each person for what they have done.
“I tell you the truth: there are some of those who are standing here who will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”
Jesus shows His glory to His disciples (Mark 9:2-13; Luke 9:28-36)
And after six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain by themselves;
And he transformed before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his garments became white as light.
And as they were coming down the mountain, behold, Moses and Elijah appeared to them, talking with Jesus.
And Peter said to Jesus, “Lord, it is good that we are here; if you wish, I will make three tents here, one for you and one for Moses and one for Elijah.”
And while he was telling me, a bright cloud covered them, and a voice from the cloud said: “This is my beloved son, in whom I am well pleased; listen to him!”
As the disciples heard this, they fell face down and were very afraid.
And Jesus came and touched them and said, “Get up, do not fear.”
And when I told them, they raised their eyes, but didn't see that it was Jesus alone.
And as they were coming down the mountain, Jesus commanded them, “Tell no one about what you have seen until I, the Son of Man, have risen from the dead.”
And the disciples asked him, “Why do the Jewish scriptures say that Elijah must come first?”
Answer them: “Truly, Elijah will come and set everything right.”
But I tell you, that Elias is already come, and they knew him not, but have done unto him whatsoever they listed. Likewise shall also the Son of man be suffered on their hands.
And at that time the disciples understood who He was speaking to them about—John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy possessed by a demon (Mark 9:14-29; Luke 9:37-43).
And as Jesus and his disciples were coming to the people, a man came and knelt before Jesus, and said:
Oh Lord, have mercy on my son, he has fits and suffers a lot, and he always falls into the fire and into the water.
And I brought it to your students, but they couldn’t cure him.
And Jesus answered and said, “O faithless and twisted generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Bring him here to me!”
And Jesus cast out the demon, and he came out of him, and the boy was healed at once.
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus aside and said, “Why could we not cast him out?”
He said to them: “Because of your weak faith! If you were to convey the truth, if you had faith the size of a mustard seed, you would tell this mountain: ‘Move from here to there!’ and it would move, and you would become capable of everything.”
# 17:21 There are ancient texts containing this verse: As for this type, it does not come out except with prayer and fasting. Jesus speaks again about his death and resurrection (Mark 9:30-32; Luke 9:43-45).
When his disciples were gathered in Galilee, Jesus said to them: “The Son of Man is about to be handed over to the hands of men,”
And they kill me, and then we rise on the third day.” My brothers, don’t worry too much. Jesus pays the temple taxes.
They went to Capernaum and approached Peter and said to him, “Does your teacher pay the temple tax?”
Answered: “Yes, yes.” And when he told me to go home, he anticipated Jesus with his words and said: “What do you think, Simon? From whom do the kings of the earth take taxes and duties? From their own people or from foreigners?”
Peter said, “From the Brazilians.” They said to Jesus, “Indeed, the sons of the country are free.”
As for us, we don’t like to cause them trouble, go to the lake and cast your line, and catch the first fish you get and when you dissect its mouth, you will find a silver coin enough to pay for you and me. Take it and pay my taxes and yours.
